$WW,1$$FG,4$$TX+CX,"Why Not More?"$$FG$
$FG,8$$TX+CX,"The sins of omission"$$FG$

If a feature cannot be made to work correctly and consistently, professional companies usually remove the feature.  Because PC hardware is so diverse, getting things to work on all people's computers is really difficult.  For one thing, you practically have to own all the different hardware to write drivers for it.  If a company wanted to sell a PC operating system, they would offer a warranty and, therefore, could not get away with amateur behavior.  AntichristOS absolutely requires 64-bit computers, so we leave behind much trouble, but plenty remains.

The PCI bus interface is what modern hardware uses.  Before PCI, life was simple and devices used I/O ports.  After studying $LK,"PCI Interrupts",A="FI:::/Demo/Lectures/PCIInterrupts.HC"$ and attempting a HDAudio driver, TempleOS concluded that modern PCI devices require ten times more code, and that they cannot be made to work on everyone's machine, because with PCI devices there are several models to worry about, unlike with the older ISA bus devices which can be done with one driver.

So there are no PCI drivers.  The drivers use I/O ports and operate in ISA bus mode.  There is only one driver for each type of device and it is delightfully simple that way: one $LK,"keyboard",A="FI:::/Kernel/SerialDev/Keyboard.HC"$ driver, one $LK,"mouse",A="FI:::/Kernel/SerialDev/Mouse.HC"$ driver, one $LK,"ATA hard drive",A="FI:::/Kernel/BlkDev/DskATA.HC"$ driver, one $LK,"ATAPI CD/DVD",A="FI:::/Kernel/BlkDev/DskATA.HC"$ driver, one $LK,"VGA 640x480 16 color",A="FI:::/Adam/Gr/GrScrn.HC"$ video driver and one $LK,"PC Speaker",A="MN:Snd"$ driver, which knows the tritone by heart.  It uses the $LK,"PIT and HPET timers",A="MN:TimersInit"$ and $LK,"PIC Interrupt Controller",A="MN:IntsInit"$: IRQ0 for timer, IRQ1 for keyboard, and IRQ12 for mouse.  If IRQ12 is not firing, it polls the mouse.

In the CPU department, there is state of the art 64-bit $LK,"long mode",A="FI:::/Kernel/KStart64.HC"$ with $LK,"multicore",A="FI:::/Kernel/MultiProc.HC"$ support, using the $LK,"APIC",A="MN:MPAPICInit"$ to start-up $LK,"multicore",A="MN:Core0StartMP"$ operation.

It works on practically everyone's computer as long as it is 64-bit and runs inside VMware, QEMU or VirtualBox.  That was an incredible accomplishment, and it is TempleOS's, not ours.

Adding a USB driver would be really ugly with UHCI, EHCI, OHCI, USB1, USB2, USB3, ICH6, ICH7, ICH8, ICH9, ICH10, ICH11, ICH12, boot mode and regular mode for keyboard/mouse and a diversity of HID reports.  It's hopeless.  It could never offer anything but limited support and it would add a ton of code that mostly didn't work.  What would be gained?  Nothing.  A keyboard or mouse would not be improved.  Solid State USB drives would be really nice, but it's not going to happen.

The same story is basically true for GPUs, audio, networking and AHCI hard drive drivers.  640x480 16 color is a pact, so the video will never change, even if a $LK,"Standard PC",A="FI:::/Doc/StdAntichristOSPC.DD"$ was made.  If you attempt multimedia, everything will break because memory will get fragmented with huge multimedia files.  Some day, if super-simple high speed serial allows networking, there will be no browser within the 100,000 line limit and, with only 16 colors, the world wide web is not tolerable.  FTP and telnet might be possible, in the far distant future, if they could fit within the 100,000 line limit.  Currently, there are $TX,"82,150",D="DD_TEMPLEOS_LOC"$ lines of code.

Native hardware is a long shot, these days.  TempleOS could install and run natively on hardware from about 2005-2010.  It requires the BIOS to provide USB mode PS/2 legacy keyboard/mouse support, and newer machines often ship with that support disabled or missing.

The ATA/ATAPI hard drives often can be run with I/O ports if you can $LK,"find them",A="FI:::/Kernel/BlkDev/DskATAId.HC"$.  $FG,2$lspci -v$FG$ on Linux or system information on Windows can help you locate the SATA IO ports the hard drive and CD/DVD have.  They are often no longer enabled by the BIOS.  Drive performance is very slow with PIO, but it works for everybody.

UEFI is pointless here.  VMware, QEMU and VirtualBox will always support non-UEFI mode.  Without working, native hard drive and CD/DVD drivers, you can't get very far with UEFI on a native install, not to mention SecureBoot.  If non-UEFI works in a virtual machine, supporting UEFI would only be redundant code.  The compiler does not create an ELF or PE format, and teaching it to would ruin its beauty.  Some things are too damned even for us.

See also the $LK,"Infernal Demands",A="FI:::/Doc/Demands.DD"$.

When the PC was created, they wanted flexibility because they did not know the future.  Now, the industry is mature and it is time to make a 100% standard PC that everybody uses.  $LK,"::/Doc/StdAntichristOSPC.DD"$

